  "answer":"When a tool (such as a search) times out, the system must not rely on any partial or invalid evidence; instead it should either abstain from answering or ask the user for clarification, following the reliability guidelines that prescribe abstention on timeout. Additionally, the reliability policy advises retrying transient errors up to three times with exponential backoff before abstaining.\n\nDrift alerts by themselves are not sufficient to justify promoting a new model to production. The release policy explicitly states that a drift alert alone never authorizes promotion, and the drift runbook clarifies that drift should only trigger challenger training and evaluation, not an immediate production change. Production model changes must be evaluated on a frozen holdout set and meet specific metric thresholds (e.g., F1 improvement ≥ 0.005 and ROC‑AUC drop ≤ 0.01) before they can be released.",
